      "study_summary": {
        "title": "Framing the Two Halves: A Spatial Study of Syndromes and a Century",
        "central_argument": "To test whether framing, camera position, and spatial repetition distinguish the film's two halves, a filmmaker must treat every remembered detail as a hypothesis and design close-viewing tasks that isolate formal variables. The critic's claim of a shift from warm, intimate rural scenes to cold, detached urban ones (C2) provides a starting point, but it remains an attributed interpretation, not an observed fact. By systematically logging shot scale, camera angle, offscreen space, and recurring compositions, the filmmaker can test whether the film's spatial grammar actually changes between halves, or whether the perceived tonal shift arises from other elements such as performance, sound, or editing rhythm. The study must avoid inventing scene details and instead convert each gap into a verifiable question, using theory sources to define what to look for and how to interpret it.",
        "section_lenses": [
          "Shot scale and camera distance",
          "Camera angle and vertical position",
          "Offscreen space and framing edges",
          "Spatial repetition and recurring compositions",
          "Camera movement and staging"
        ],
