      "study_summary": {
        "title": "Framing Mistrust: Testing Uncertainty in The Thing",
        "central_argument": "Because no clip evidence is supplied, every formal claim about The Thing must remain a viewing hypothesis. The film’s reputation for paranoia invites a study of how framing, eyelines, and editing could organise uncertainty and group mistrust, but the analyst must separate what the record supports from what the viewer must verify. Drawing on continuity editing, constructive editing, vector-line principles, and montage theory, this essay proposes a sequence of testable relations: how shot/reverse-shot patterns may withhold or misdirect eyelines, how screen-direction violations may destabilise spatial trust, how constructive editing may fragment group space, and how rhythmic cutting may escalate suspicion. Each section advances a distinct formal relation, cites a framework source, and converts the gap between theory and film into a precise close-viewing task.",
